      "id": 1,
      "text": "Papineau was named after Louis‑Joseph Papineau, a Quebec political leader, reflecting its French‑Canadian heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        6,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bradleyillinois.com/towns/papineau.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village sits on the Kankakee‑Iroquois County line, giving it a unique border identity between two counties.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        26,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bradleyillinois.com/towns/papineau.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Founded in the mid‑1800s, Papineau grew as an agricultural community worked by French‑Canadian and other farming families.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bradleyillinois.com/towns/papineau.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "With roughly 200 residents, Papineau remains a tight‑knit prairie town where everyone knows each other.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bradleyillinois.com/towns/papineau.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Papineau’s location 15 miles north of Watseka and 16 miles southeast of Kankakee places it in northeastern Iroquois County.",
